Oregano is often referred to as the "pizza spice" or a Greek herb. It gets these connotations because it grows wild in the mountains of Greece and Italy. In Greek, the word "oregano" means "joy of the mountain."
&#xD;
Oregano goes particularly well with tomatoes, aubergine, and lamb and is generally added just at the end of cooking, so that it retains its pungency. Chop and use it&#xA0;to top pizzas, add to Greek salad, or use a large bunch as a bed on which to roast a joint.

Thyme's intensely pungent flavor complements most meats, including chicken and game. Its robust nature means that it can withstand long cooking times and it is a good complement to slow-cooked dishes such as stews and daubes.&#xA0;
&#xD;
It is one of the herbs used in bouquet gami, along with parsley and bay. Its flavor also marries well with other robust and ready herbs such as rosemary and sage. Chop it up in stuffings for poultry or lamb, or use it chopped in a marinade for olives. Add springs to marinades for meat, fish, or vegetables, or tuck a few springs with half a lemon and an onion inside a chicken before roasting.

